Hamilton Anxiety Scale (HAMA) scores on day 10 and day 90 were \<, respectively; Proportion of patients with scores of 7, ≥7, and ≥14.The scale used a 5-level scoring method, and the criteria for each level were: 0-asymptomatic; 1- Light; 2- Medium; 3- heavy; 4- Extremely heavy. The total score of Hamilton anxiety Scale is a very important basis to reflect the severity of patients' anxiety, that is, the less severe the disease, the lower the total score; The more severe the condition, the higher the total score. A total score of less than 7 is considered to have no symptoms of anxiety; A score of 7-13 is considered to be likely to have anxiety; A score of 14 to 20 is considered to be definitely anxious; 21 to 28 points, is considered to have significant anxiety; A score greater than 29 is considered likely to be severe anxiety.

